>
  <h1>How to Make a Paper Fortune Teller</h1>
  <p class="updated">A classic playground game, explained simply</p>

  <p>
    The paper fortune teller — sometimes called a cootie catcher, salt cellar, or chatterbox
    — is a simple folded-paper game that's been popular in classrooms and playgrounds for
    generations. Here's exactly how to make one, decorate it, and play it.
  </p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One square piece of paper (any size, printer paper trimmed to a square works fine)</li>
    <li>A pen, pencil, or markers</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to make a paper fortune teller, step by step</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Fold the square in half diagonally, corner to corner, then unfold. Repeat with the other diagonal, so you have an X-shaped crease.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Fold all four corners into the center point, so you get a smaller square.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Flip the paper over. Fold all four corners into the center again.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Fold the whole square in half, then unfold, then fold in half the other way — this creates the pocket lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Slide your thumbs and index fingers under the four flaps on the bottom side and gently push them together, so the fortune teller can open and close like a mouth on both axes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Making a Chinese-style fortune teller</h2>
  <p>
    The fold is identical to the standard version above — the "Chinese fortune teller" and
    "Chinese fortune telling paper game" names simply refer to the same origami fold as it's
    traditionally taught and played in parts of East Asia, usually decorated with numbers,
    colours, and short fortunes exactly as described below.
  </p>

  <h2>What to write inside</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Outside flaps (4):</strong> Write one colour on each — e.g. Red, Blue, Green, Yellow.</li>
    <li><strong>Inside triangles (8):</strong> Number them 1 through 8.</li>
    <li><strong>Under each numbered flap:</strong> Write a short fortune, joke, dare, or fun fact — for example "You'll get great news this week" or "Sing your favourite song."</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to play</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask a friend to pick one of the four colours shown on the outside.</li>
    <li>Open and close the fortune teller once for each letter as you spell that colour out loud.</li>
    <li>Ask them to pick one of the numbers now showing inside, and repeat the open-and-close motion that many times.</li>
    <li>Ask them to pick one final number, then lift that flap and read their fortune aloud.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tips for coloring and decorating</h2>
  <p>
    Give each of the four outer flaps a distinct solid colour so they're easy to tell apart
    once folded. Some people like to draw small symbols or doodles matching each colour's
    theme (e.g. a sun for yellow, a wave for blue) to make picking more fun for younger
    players.
  </p>

  <p style="font-size:12px; color:#6b6880; margin-top:30px;">
